Think like a contrarian. CNBC says a 10% correction is coming because “that’s what always happens” before midterms. 

Goldman says hedge funds are the most deleveraged they’ve been all year. 

Sounds bearish. But then ask: who is left to sell? You need sellers for a 10% drawdown. https://t.co/wNIfCwlYth

Don’t ask me if I’m bullish or bearish. I have no opinion on what the market does over the next month. But one thing I do know: when everybody is expecting a 10% correction, it usually doesn’t happen.

@GordianKnotDev The positioning is what makes this interesting, not the rhetoric. Hedge funds have already almost fully deleveraged. I’m looking at where people are actually positioned, not at the endless talking and meaningless opinions. https://t.co/hmr6IWYI5S

@Matans8442 There’s a video attached in English, and if you have access to bank research reports, they discuss this there as well. If you don’t understand the language in the video, maybe we can find a Hebrew version, or ChatGPT can probably translate it into Hebrew for you.

@StratsLabs That’s my base case as well, and I structure the ratio spreads accordingly, with an extra margin of safety, as I’ll explain in the next tweet.

@NoelKapela Bear in mind, markets tend to be less reactive to things that have happened before. Known knows are rarely what brings  markets down hard.

